TEST 12589 -- Interoperability with RIPv1/v2 (Policy Based Routing)
===========================================================================
Run:    2026-10-01 09:00-09:45 NZDT, tb470, bench-runner (queue CAMPAIGN-QUEUE-2026-09-29.md
        row 16, sentinel device-testing-67). Hand-driven: no framework script.
Build:  awplus_main-20260923-20 (stack and IE520-sa); x230 awplus_5.5.5_2-20260918-7
DUT:    IE520 3-member VCStack "IE520-stk" (members 1/3/4, master member 3), router MAC
        0000.cd37.0d6f (virtual). RIP peers: the standalone IE520-sa and the x230-10GP.
Case:   AWPTCM-T12589 (ck.db, folder "/Policy Based Routing/Policy based Routing - x510").
        Objective "Confirm that Policy-based Routing and RIPv1/v2 work correctly each other."
        IXIA1 packets: Pkts1 = source 192.168.10.1-127 (NOT matching the traffic class),
        Pkts2 = source 192.168.10.128-254 (matching). Steps:
          1 transmit Pkt1+Pkt2          -> Pkts1 to VLAN40, Pkts2 to VLAN30
          2 VLAN30 down, transmit       -> Pkts1 and Pkts2 to VLAN40
          3 VLAN30 up, transmit         -> Pkts1 to VLAN40, Pkts2 to VLAN30
          4 invalid PBR next-hop, transmit -> Pkts1 and Pkts2 to VLAN40
          5 RIP version 2 -> 1 on all switches, repeat 1-4 -> same results
        The environment and switch config are "See attachment file"; the attachment is not in
        ck.db, so the topology below is built from the steps (VLAN30 = PBR next hop, VLAN40 =
        the RIP route). Scope: Terrence, relayed 2026-09-30 15:50, "yes" to the full build.

VERDICT: FAIL.
  PBR and RIP interoperate while the PBR next hop is reachable AND already in the DUT's ARP
  table: steps 1 and 3 give exactly the expected split under RIPv2 and RIPv1 (5,080/5,080 of
  each kind, nothing on the wrong path). Whenever the next hop is NOT usable, the DUT does not
  fall back to the routing table (the RIP route via VLAN40), as steps 2 and 4 expect.
  Instead it routes every PBR-matched packet out of the INGRESS port, untagged in vlan1, to
  the MAC of an unrelated host: tb470 eth1, 00f0.4d00.7716 = 10.38.215.1, the first entry in
  the stack's ARP table.
    step 2 (VLAN30 SVI shut)                   v2 FAIL, v1 FAIL
    step 4 (next-hop 172.16.30.99, in-subnet, no host) v2 FAIL, v1 FAIL
    step 4 (next-hop 10.99.99.1, no route)     v2 FAIL, v1 FAIL
    step 3 (VLAN30 up)                         FAIL as the step reads: the misdirection
      continues after `no shutdown`, because the DUT never re-resolves the next hop by
      itself. Correct (5,080/5,080 to VLAN30) only once the next hop's ARP entry exists
      (primed with `ping 172.16.30.2` from the DUT); v2 and v1 alike.
  Pkts1 (not matched) were routed correctly by RIP to VLAN40 in every run, 5,080/5,080.
  RIPv1 and RIPv2 both work between the IE520 stack, the IE520-sa and the x230, and the
  version change makes no difference to the PBR result.

  Two defects for review:
  D-1 PBR with an unusable next hop (down, unresolved, or with no route) misroutes matched
      traffic to an unrelated neighbour's MAC in another VLAN, out of the ingress port. It
      does not fall back to the routing table and does not drop. Effects: traffic goes to the
      wrong host, crosses a VLAN boundary, and here it escaped the lab through tb470 (below).
  D-2 PBR does not trigger ARP resolution of its next hop. After `no shutdown` of the next
      hop's VLAN (which flushes the entry), PBR stays broken (it misroutes per D-1) until
      something else makes the DUT ARP the next hop. Observed: no traffic ever
      re-resolves it. A steady-state entry did NOT age out in 12 min with no traffic
      (12589-arpwatch.txt).

