INCIDENT -- misrouted test traffic left the lab through tb470 (09:10-09:14)
  tb470 has ip_forward=1 and loose rp_filter (eth1 = 2), and its default route is via
  10.36.255.254 (dev network). The Pkts2 the DUT misrouted to eth1's MAC were therefore
  forwarded out tb470's uplink: 5,080 in each of the two v2 step-2 runs, plus 1 canary
  frame. FORWARD chain policy ACCEPT counter = 10,161 packets / 1,117,710 bytes. They were
  124-byte UDP packets from spoofed sources 192.168.10.128-254 to 172.16.89.2, over ~20 s at
  500 pps. Nothing else reached the uplink.
  Reported to the sentinel at 09:12 (NEEDS TERRENCE); steps that could misroute were held.
  Terrence (relayed): "did (b), continue". He added, on tb470:
    -A FORWARD -s 192.168.10.0/24 -j DROP        (first rule in FORWARD)
  From then on every run was bracketed and proved: the rule's own packet counter, and a
  capture on dev network for src 192.168.10.0/24 beside an unfiltered liveness capture:
    run            DROP rule pkts        uplink src 192.168.10.x
    v1 step 2 #1   0 -> 5080             (no uplink capture yet)
    v1 step 2 #2   5080 -> 10160         0
    v1 step 4a     10160 -> 15240        0
    v1 step 4b     15240 -> 20320        0
    v2 step 4 pre  20320 -> 20320        0 of 501 uplink frames
    v2 step 4a     20320 -> 25400        0 of 448
    v2 step 4b     25400 -> 30480        0 of 587
    v2 restored    30480 -> 30480        0 of 587
  FORWARD's ACCEPT policy and DOCKER-USER counters stayed at 10,161 (their pre-rule value)
  throughout, so no 192.168.10.x packet got past the DROP. NB: on this kernel (6.12)
  IpForwDatagrams counts forward ATTEMPTS before the FORWARD hook, so it rose by 5,080
  per run even while the rule dropped them; it is not evidence of a leak.
  THE RULE IS STILL IN PLACE (30,480 hits at 09:45). It is Terrence's to remove.

